{{- /* Everything inside <body>: the app bar, the layout, the toasts and the topics dialog.
Rendered on page load by shell.gohtml, and patched WHOLE on every interaction ("fat morph").

The Tao of Datastar: "Morphing ensures that only modified parts of the DOM are updated,
preserving state and improving performance. This allows you to send down large chunks of the
DOM tree". So this is the unit of update, and the server stops having to remember which
fragments a change touches -- the class of bug that let a clamped `difficulty` sit stale in
the sidebar.

Two things stay OUTSIDE this region, on <body>, precisely because they must not be
re-created by a morph: the signal declarations, and `data-init` (which in stream mode opens
the timer connection -- re-running it per patch would leak a held connection each time). */ -}}
    <header class="topbar">
      <button type="button" class="nav-toggle" aria-label="Toggle sidebar"
              data-attr:aria-expanded="$_navOpen"
              data-on:click="$_navOpen = !$_navOpen">
        <span class="bars" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      </button>
      <h1>{{ .Variant.Title }}</h1>
      {{- /* The theme toggle, on the CHROME side of the bar -- left of the spacer, with the
      hamburger. Not in the drawer, because the drawer starts closed and "this is too bright
      right now" is fixed the moment it is noticed. Not in the HUD either: the bar is two
      clusters split by `.topbar-spacer`, chrome on the left and game state on the right, and
      a palette is not a move in the game. It also should not neighbour Skip -- that is the
      one control that SPENDS something.

      Three states in one button: auto (the OS decides, the default) -> light -> dark. Auto
      is a real state, not a synonym for light: it is the only one that follows the machine
      when it switches at sunset, and it is what `data-theme` being ABSENT means. The glyphs
      are symbol-font TEXT, not emoji, so they take the app bar's white. */ -}}
      <button type="button" class="theme-toggle button"
              data-attr:aria-label="'Colour theme: ' + $_theme + '. Activate to change.'"
              data-attr:title="'Theme: ' + $_theme"
              data-on:click="$_theme = $_theme === 'auto' ? 'light' : ($_theme === 'light' ? 'dark' : 'auto');
                document.cookie = '{{ .ThemeCookie }}=' + $_theme + ';path={{ .CookiePath }};max-age=31536000;samesite=lax'">
        <span aria-hidden="true"
              data-text="$_theme === 'light' ? '☀' : ($_theme === 'dark' ? '☾' : '◐')">◐</span>
      </button>
      <span class="topbar-spacer"></span>

      {{- /* THE HUD: live state and the one action you take *during* a question.

      Sorted by when you touch it: live state and Skip live here, always visible; anything
      that restarts the quiz lives in the drawer, which starts closed.

      The streak GROWS with the run and warms through two colour bands. `Math.min` caps the
      growth at 8 -- an unbounded scale eventually reflows the app bar. It SAYS "streak",
      because a bare "3x" is a rebus; the word is dropped again under 560px, where the bar
      has no room for it, and the `aria-label` carries it at every width. */ -}}
      <span class="streak"
            data-attr:aria-label="'streak ' + $_streak"
            data-class="{hot: $_streak >= 3, blazing: $_streak >= 6, cold: $_streak < 1}"
            data-style:transform="'scale(' + (1 + Math.min($_streak, 8) * 0.06) + ')'">
        <span class="streak-label" aria-hidden="true">streak</span>
        <span aria-hidden="true" data-text="$_streak"></span><span aria-hidden="true">&times;</span>
      </span>

      {{- /* The fraction is wrapped so the CSS can shed it on a narrow bar: under 400px the
      bar keeps the POINTS, because points are what the skips are earned against. */ -}}
      <span class="topbar-score">
        <span class="score-fraction"><span data-text="$_correct"></span>/<span data-text="$_attempted"></span>&nbsp;·&nbsp;</span><span data-text="$_points"></span> pts
      </span>

      {{- /* The points gauge, promoted out of the sidebar. The NOTCHES are why it earns space
      up here: each one is a skip you can earn, so the bar answers "how close am I to another
      skip" at a glance. The gradient stays on the track with a mask over the unearned part,
      so a colour means the same number of points wherever the bar has reached. */ -}}
      <div class="meter points-meter hud-meter" role="img"
           data-class="{full: $_pointsPct >= 100}"
           data-attr:aria-label="'points ' + $_points + ' of {{ .PointsGoal }}'">
        <div class="meter-mask" data-style:width="(100 - $_pointsPct) + '%'"></div>
        {{- range .MilestoneTicks }}
        <span class="meter-tick" style="left: {{ . }}%"
              data-class="{earned: $_pointsPct >= {{ . }}}"></span>
        {{- end }}
      </div>

      {{- /* Skip: an in-play action, so it is in the HUD rather than the drawer. `s` works
      too -- same guards as the digit accelerators, plus the button's own disabled conditions
      restated, because a window handler does not inherit them. */ -}}
      <button type="button" class="skip warning button is-warning"
              data-attr:disabled="$_skipsLeft <= 0 || !$_playing"
              data-on:click="@post('{{ .Prefix }}/skip{{ .VariantQuery }}')"
              data-on:keydown__window="evt.key === 's'
                && $_skipsLeft > 0 && $_playing && !$_answering && !$_topicsOpen
                && !evt.target.closest?.('{{ .TypingTargets }}')
                && @post('{{ .Prefix }}/skip{{ .VariantQuery }}')">
        Skip <span class="skip-count" data-text="$_skipsLeft"></span>
      </button>
    </header>

    <div class="layout" data-class="{'nav-closed': !$_navOpen}">
      {{- /* Dismiss-on-outside-click, but NOT when the click was the toggle itself: the
      hamburger sits outside the drawer, so opening it also counted as an outside click and
      the two handlers cancelled each other out. */ -}}
      <aside class="sidebar"
             data-on:click__outside="$_navOpen && window.matchMedia('{{ .DrawerOverlayQuery }}').matches && !evt.target.closest('.nav-toggle') && ($_navOpen = false)">
        {{- /* What is left of the old `#score` panel: the dial, in a collapsed group. The
        numbers and the points gauge moved to the HUD; the dial stays because it is pleasant
        and panel had one, but it spends a lot of space on a single number.

        `data-preserve-attr="open"`: `open` is state the PLAYER set, and the server has no
        idea about it -- so a fat morph, which rewrites `#app` from markup that never carries
        the attribute, snapped every disclosure shut on every answer, skip and restart. */ -}}
        <details class="panel box progress" id="progress" data-preserve-attr="open">
          <summary>Progress</summary>
          {{- /* The panel Dial, by hand. One 90-radius arc, coloured by band and revealed
          with stroke-dashoffset -- so the value is a signal expression and no markup is
          patched. `PI * 90 = 282.74` is the full sweep. */ -}}
          <svg class="dial" viewBox="0 0 200 110" role="img" aria-label="percentage correct">
            <path class="dial-track" d="M 10 100 A 90 90 0 0 1 190 100" />
            <path class="dial-value" d="M 10 100 A 90 90 0 0 1 190 100"
                  data-style:stroke-dashoffset="282.74 * (1 - $_scorePct / 100)"
                  data-class="{poor: $_scorePct < 30, weak: $_scorePct >= 30 && $_scorePct < 49, fair: $_scorePct >= 49 && $_scorePct < 59, ok: $_scorePct >= 59 && $_scorePct < 75, good: $_scorePct >= 75}" />
            <text class="dial-text" x="100" y="95" data-text="$_scorePct + '%'">0%</text>
          </svg>
          <p class="score-line">
            <span data-text="$_correct"></span> of <span data-text="$_attempted"></span> right
            &middot; <span data-text="$_points"></span> / {{ .PointsGoal }} points
          </p>
        </details>

        {{- /* The debug panel: for reaching states that take minutes of honest play. Rendered
        only when the session is armed -- `?debug` on a page load, or DSQUIZ_DEBUG=1. Not
        styled beyond a warning tint on purpose: it should look like scaffolding. */ -}}
        {{- if .Debug }}
        <section class="panel box debug" id="debug">
          <strong>Debug</strong>
          <div class="debug-row">
            <button type="button" class="button is-small" data-on:click="@post('{{ .Prefix }}/debug/points/100{{ .VariantQuery }}')">+100 pts</button>
            <button type="button" class="button is-small" data-on:click="@post('{{ .Prefix }}/debug/points/-100{{ .VariantQuery }}')">&minus;100</button>
          </div>
          <div class="debug-row">
            {{- /* the goal decides completion AND where the skip milestones fall, so a small
            goal exercises the whole ladder in a minute */ -}}
            <button type="button" class="button is-small" data-on:click="@post('{{ .Prefix }}/debug/goal/200{{ .VariantQuery }}')">goal 200</button>
            <button type="button" class="button is-small" data-on:click="@post('{{ .Prefix }}/debug/goal/1000{{ .VariantQuery }}')">goal 1000</button>
          </div>
          <div class="debug-row">
            <button type="button" class="button is-small" data-on:click="@post('{{ .Prefix }}/debug/reveal{{ .VariantQuery }}')">show reveal</button>
            <button type="button" class="button is-small" data-on:click="@post('{{ .Prefix }}/debug/complete{{ .VariantQuery }}')">show finale</button>
          </div>
          <p class="hint">
            goal {{ .PointsGoal }} &middot; qid {{ .QID }} &middot;
            {{ if .Playing }}playing{{ else }}finished{{ end }} &middot; build {{ .BuildStamp }}
          </p>
        </section>
        {{- end }}

        <section class="panel box controls">
          <label>
            Difficulty <output data-text="$difficulty"></output>
            {{- /* `change`, not `input`: fires on release, like panel's value_throttled --
            but debounced, because with the KEYBOARD `change` fires on every arrow keypress
            and a settings change restarts the quiz, so holding an arrow key wiped the score.
            `value` is server-rendered too: a range input with no value defaults to the
            midpoint of its span, which is what the browser paints before datastar's binding
            has run. */ -}}
            <input type="range" min="{{ .MinDifficulty }}" max="{{ .MaxDifficulty }}" step="1"
                   value="{{ .Settings.Difficulty }}"
                   data-bind:difficulty data-on:change__debounce.400ms="@post('{{ .Prefix }}/settings{{ .VariantQuery }}')" />
          </label>
          {{- /* `checked` is server-rendered for the same reason as the slider `value`s, and
          it matters more here: an unchecked box on first paint is state the binding would
          push back up, quietly turning ladder mode off. */ -}}
          <label class="checkbox">
            <input type="checkbox" {{ if .Settings.LadderMode }}checked {{ end }}
                   data-bind:ladder-mode data-on:change="@post('{{ .Prefix }}/settings{{ .VariantQuery }}')" />
            Ladder mode (can lose points)
          </label>
          <label class="checkbox">
            <input type="checkbox" {{ if .Settings.TargetOn }}checked {{ end }}
                   data-bind:target-on data-on:change="@post('{{ .Prefix }}/settings{{ .VariantQuery }}')" />
            Target percentage required
          </label>
          <label>
            Target <output data-text="$targetPct"></output>%
            <input type="range" min="70" max="90" step="10" value="{{ .Settings.TargetPct }}"
                   data-attr:disabled="!$targetOn"
                   data-bind:target-pct data-on:change__debounce.400ms="@post('{{ .Prefix }}/settings{{ .VariantQuery }}')" />
          </label>
          {{- /* Topics before the filter, and the filter folded away behind "Advanced",
          because they answer the same question at two very different prices: a topic is a
          name you recognise, the filter is a pattern language with six rules. The STATUS line
          stays outside the fold: it says what the working set currently is. */ -}}
          {{- if .Topics }}
          {{- /* Opening the picker closes the drawer on a narrow screen: the button is IN the
          drawer, so on a phone you would otherwise be looking at a dialog stacked on top of
          the thing you opened it from. */ -}}
          <button type="button" class="light button is-light"
                  data-on:click="$_topicsOpen = true; window.matchMedia('{{ .DrawerOverlayQuery }}').matches && ($_navOpen = false)">Topics&hellip;</button>
          {{- end }}
          <div class="filter-status" id="filter-status">{{ .FilterStatus }}</div>
          <details class="advanced" data-preserve-attr="open">
            <summary>Advanced: bidding tree filter</summary>
            {{- /* `$filterText` is the *draft* -- typed but not applied -- which is client
            state by definition. Every keystroke asks the server what it would select; only
            Enter commits. The validation stays where the matcher is, so the browser knows
            nothing about bidding. */ -}}
            <label>
              Bidding tree filter
              <input type="text" class="input" list="topic-names" placeholder="e.g. 1D-1M-1N, or a topic"
                     value="{{ .FilterText }}"
                     data-bind:filter-text
                     data-on:input__debounce.300ms="@get('{{ .Prefix }}/filter/preview{{ .VariantQuery }}')"
                     data-on:keydown="evt.key === 'Enter' && (evt.target.blur(), @post('{{ .Prefix }}/filter/apply{{ .VariantQuery }}'))" />
            </label>
            {{- /* native autocomplete: the topic names, no javascript of ours */ -}}
            <datalist id="topic-names">
              {{- range .Topics }}
              <option value="{{ .Name }}"></option>
              {{- end }}
            </datalist>
            <details class="filter-help" data-preserve-attr="open">
              <summary>Filter syntax</summary>
              <ul>
                <li><code>1D-1M-1N</code> a bid; suits C D H S N. Separate calls with a dash or a
                  space &mdash; <code>1D 1H</code>, <code>1D-1H</code> and <code>1D--1H</code> are the same</li>
                <li><code>M</code> / <code>m</code> any major / any minor &mdash; <em>the only place case matters</em></li>
                <li><code>1*</code> / <code>*</code> any suit at that level / any call</li>
                <li><code>Pass</code> <code>X</code> <code>XX</code> pass, double, redouble</li>
                <li><code>(2H)</code> brackets = the opponents' call; <code>(*)</code> = they did something</li>
                <li><code>1D-1M, 2C</code> comma separates alternatives &mdash; either one matches</li>
              </ul>
              <p>
                A pattern describes <strong>your</strong> auction: opponent calls you do not mention
                are stepped over, so <code>1D-1H</code> matches 1D (Pass) 1H, 1D (1S) 1H and
                1D (X) 1H. Bracket a call to pin the opponents down at that exact point. Too few
                matches falls back to the whole system.
              </p>
            </details>
          </details>

          <p class="hint"><em>Changes restart the quiz.</em></p>
          {{- /* Restart also CLOSES the drawer, but only where the drawer is an overlay.
          Below 900px it covers the quiz, so "start again" that leaves you looking at the
          settings panel you just used is a second tap for no reason. Only the explicit
          button does this -- the sliders and checkboxes restart the quiz too, and you may be
          adjusting several. */ -}}
          <button type="button" class="danger button is-danger"
                  data-on:click="@post('{{ .Prefix }}/restart{{ .VariantQuery }}');
                    window.matchMedia('{{ .DrawerOverlayQuery }}').matches && ($_navOpen = false)">Restart</button>
          {{- /* Presentation, not quiz rules -- and collapsed, because the settings column
          was the tallest thing on the page. `data-bind` in the *value* form: both signal
          names start with an underscore. */ -}}
          <details class="appearance" data-preserve-attr="open">
            <summary>Appearance</summary>
            {{- /* Both pickers hand focus BACK after they commit. A <select> keeps focus once
            clicked, and the digit accelerators ignore keystrokes aimed at a form control --
            so picking a font or a stylesheet silently disabled 1-9 for the rest of the
            session. These two are safe to blur because a change is the whole interaction; the
            difficulty slider and the filter box are NOT. */ -}}
            {{- /* The Bulma option is the reason for the `class="light button is-light"`
            pairs throughout these templates: Bulma is class-based, so the framework's class
            strings have to be IN the markup, and they ship on every patch whichever
            stylesheet is active. That is the cost the comparison is measuring, so it is paid
            in the open. DEBUG ONLY: the A/B/C is settled and the three variants differ in
            details a player has no way to care about. */ -}}
            {{- if .Debug }}
            <label>
              Base CSS
              {{- /* Bulma wants `<div class="select">` AROUND the select -- a wrapper element,
              not a class on the select. A plain div for the other two sheets, which is
              exactly the kind of markup tax that does not show up in a line count. */ -}}
              <div class="select">
                <select data-bind="_css" data-on:change="evt.target.blur()">
                  <option value="hand">Hand-rolled</option>
                  <option value="pico">Pico classless</option>
                  <option value="bulma">Bulma (spike)</option>
                </select>
              </div>
            </label>
            {{- end }}
            {{- /* The game-feel experiment, on by default. `checked` is rendered statically to
            match the declared default of `$_juice`: an unchecked box would be what
            `data-bind` uploads into the signal on first paint, turning it off before anyone
            chose to. */ -}}
            <label class="checkbox">
              <input type="checkbox" checked data-bind="_juice" />
              Game feel (shake, floating points, streak)
            </label>
            {{- /* Sound is the one appearance preference that starts OFF, and NOT for the
            usual conservative reason: the others change how the page looks to the person who
            asked for them, while audio arrives in whatever room the laptop is in. Note the
            missing `checked` -- it has to agree with the declared default of `$_sound`, or
            the first paint would upload `true` into the signal and the quiz would start
            making noise on its own. */ -}}
            <label class="checkbox">
              <input type="checkbox" data-bind="_sound" />
              Sound (countdown tick, scoring chimes)
            </label>
            <label>
              Font
              <div class="select">
                <select data-bind="_font" data-on:change="evt.target.blur()">
                <option value="notes">Open Sans (as the notes)</option>
                <option value="system">System UI</option>
                <option value="rounded">Rounded</option>
                <option value="serif">Serif</option>
                <option value="mono">Monospace</option>
                </select>
              </div>
            </label>
          </details>
        </section>
      </aside>

      <main class="main">
        {{- /* the one fragment that is patched as elements. `card` is OUR class and also one
        of Bulma's -- and for once they mean the same thing, so the Bulma variant inherits its
        surface for free.

        COMING BACK FROM THE SYSTEM NOTES TAKES THE KEYBOARD BACK. Read the notes, click
        inside them to scroll, come back to the quiz, and 1-9 do nothing while the mouse still
        works. Nothing here is broken when that happens -- the notes are an <iframe>, so a
        click inside them puts focus in ANOTHER DOCUMENT, and every accelerator here is a
        `__window` listener on ours. Bringing the pointer back to the question is the gesture
        that means "I am playing again", so that is where the focus is taken back. Narrow on
        purpose -- only when the thing holding focus is an iframe -- and `window.focus()` as
        well as the blur, because the parent document has to be the one holding focus. */ -}}
        <section class="card" id="quiz"
                 data-on:mouseenter="document.activeElement?.tagName === 'IFRAME'
                   && (document.activeElement.blur(), window.focus())">{{ .QuizBody }}</section>

        {{- /* The timer's markup never changes; only the signal does. Which side produces that
        signal is the experiment (DSQUIZ_TIMER):

        client -- the browser walks $_timeLeftPct down from the allowance the server stated
                  with the question. No connection held, no per-tick server work.
        stream -- a held SSE connection pushes the value, which is panel's model exactly.

        Either way nothing here is trusted for scoring: the bonus is recomputed server-side
        from the question's start time when the answer arrives.

        In stream mode the connection is opened by `data-init` on <body>, NOT here: this
        element is inside the morph target, so a per-patch `data-init` would open a fresh held
        connection on every interaction.

        The interval gates on `$_ticking`, the server's "a live, unanswered question is being
        timed", not on `$_playing` -- which is only false once the whole quiz is over, so the
        bar kept draining after a question was answered. Assigning ONLY while ticking is what
        freezes the bar rather than resetting it.

        The last three seconds also TICK, when sound is on. It rides this interval rather than
        having one of its own: the rate limit is the SAMPLE -- the blip is padded with silence
        out to a full second, and play() on an element that is already playing does nothing.
        `$_timeLeftPct * $_questionMs < 300000` is "under three seconds left" without a
        division. */ -}}
        <div class="timer" id="timer"
             {{ if ne .TimerMode "stream" }}data-on-interval__duration.100ms="$_ticking && !$_answering && ($_timeLeftPct = Math.max(0, $_timeLeftPct - 10000 / $_questionMs)) && $_sound && $_timeLeftPct * $_questionMs < 300000 && document.getElementById('sfx-tick')?.play()?.catch(() => {})"{{ end }}>
          {{- /* `ticking` is the same condition the interval assigns under, and it is here for
          the game-feel layer: the last band throbs, and a bar that is FROZEN behind a reveal
          must not -- urgency about a question you have already answered is a lie. */ -}}
          <div class="timer-fill" data-style:width="$_timeLeftPct + '%'"
               data-class="{spent: $_timeLeftPct < 17, low: $_timeLeftPct >= 17 && $_timeLeftPct < 49, mid: $_timeLeftPct >= 49 && $_timeLeftPct < 65, high: $_timeLeftPct >= 65, ticking: $_ticking && !$_answering}"></div>
        </div>

        <div class="toasts" id="toasts" aria-live="polite"></div>

        {{ if .Topics }}{{ template "topics.gohtml" . }}{{ end }}

        <details class="notes" data-preserve-attr="open">
          <summary>Notation</summary>
          <ul>
            <li>Bids in brackets e.g (1♥), (bid), (any), (1NT) etc. indicate the opponents made the bid.</li>
            <li>The opponents' bids are often automatically removed from the question</li>
            <li>~ means roughly/approximately (points are guides, not absolute)</li>
            <li>X or Dbl means double</li>
            <li>GF/FG means game forcing</li>
            <li>NF means non-forcing</li>
            <li>M means major, oM other major</li>
            <li>m means minor, om other minor</li>
            <li>Hx/HHx means Honour + x (small card), Honour Honour x etc.</li>
          </ul>
        </details>

        <details class="notes" data-preserve-attr="open">
          <summary>System Notes</summary>
          <iframe src="{{ .Variant.SystemNotesURL }}" title="system notes" loading="lazy"></iframe>
        </details>
      </main>
    </div>
